      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note. The dose is also high enough that I want a second check before it goes in.\n\n- **Dose:** 5,100 mg/day is about double the labeled maximum (2,550 mg/day). Metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus at about 2,000 mg/day, so higher doses add little benefit. They do raise the risk of GI toxicity and lactic acidosis.\n- **Single dose:** 1700 mg per administration is far above the usual 1,000 mg ceiling.\n- **Nothing to check against:** Normal renal and hepatic function lowers the risk, but it doesn't make this dose safe.\n\nThese options would let you move forward:\n1. **I enter the maximum standard dose now.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). You can adjust it later.\n2. **Pharmacy and the specialist verify the dose first.** Once they've documented it directly, you or a pharmacist can enter the order through the usual override process. I can enter it then with a note such as \"Dose above labeled max, per [specialist], pharmacy verified.\"\n3. **Look at other options.** If standard metformin has failed, a specialist may prefer adding or switching to another agent rather than raising the metformin dose.\n\nTell me if you want me to enter option 1 or something else."
      }
     ],
